$ 
$ Basis set library file automatically generatered by basis_set.py
$ 
$ invocation: basis_set.py --file=../basis/reference_edited/6d_tz_archive --print_polarization --print_scfbas --uncontracted --equal_exponent_threshold=0.05 --polarization_type=valence correlating,6d dipole polarization,5f correlating --output=dyall.6d.tz.bas
$ 
$ These are the sets of functions selected from the TCA archive (apart from SCF):
$  ['valence correlating', '6d dipole polarization', '5f correlating']
$ 
$ dyall.6d.tz.bas
$
$ el contained in the file 
$ Cn Hs Db Mt Rf Rg Sg Ds Bh
$ 
$ 6d-Block Triple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ ======================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 12 Nov 2009
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, in preparation.
$ Available from the Dirac web site, http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the double-zeta basis sets described in the above article.  
$ 
$ The primitive basis set is a 32s 29p 20d 13f set, including exponents for the 7p.
$ 
$ NOTE: Exponents are counted from the smallest to the largest. 
$ 
$ The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * Optimization of the s, p, d, and f sets separately, on the total energy of the
$   d^n s^2 ground-state configuration
$ * Addition of 2 exponents for the 7p, optimized with reoptimization of the
$   exponents for the 3p-6p outer antinodes on the total energy of the d^n-1 s^2 p^1
$   configuration, with the innermost 15 exponents fixed.
$ * Replacement of the smallest SCF exponent in the f set with the two correlating f
$   exponents, and reoptimization of the other 11 f exponents with the correlating f
$   exponents fixed.
$ 
$ In all the following descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF 
$ exponent set. 
$ 
$ The correlating exponents were generated as follows
$ 
$ * A 2f1g exponent set for correlation of the 6d and 7s was optimized in MR-SDCI
$   calculations on the ground configuration.
$ * A 2g1h exponent set for 5f correlation was optimized in MR-DCI calculations on
$   the ground configuration, with double excitations from the 5f shell into the 
$   correlating 2g1h space coupled to J=0.
$ 
$ The exponents for 6d dipole polarization were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * The polarizing f and g exponents were generated by multiplying the smallest
$   6d correlating f and g exponents by the ratio of the 6d polarizing f exponent
$   to the 6d correlating f exponent from the double-zeta basis set.
$ 
$ 
$ The contractions include the 7p orbital and are taken from SCF calculations on
$ the configuration average of the d^n s^2 and d^n s^1 p^1 configurations. (This
$ average also covers the configurations d^n+1 s^1 and d^n-1 s^2 p^1, which are
$ also low-lying.)
$ 
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
$    of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
$    component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
$    symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
$    those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
$    The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
$    j=l+1/2.
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of valence correlating functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of 6d dipole polarization functions.
$ 
$ 5. Exponents of 5f core correlating functions.
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ 
$ Contracted basis sets can be constructed by adding primitive functions to the SCF 
$ set. These basis sets are described below. Note that the term "triple zeta" applies 
$ to the s and p set; the d set is quadruple zeta for the 6d. This is a result of 
$ keeping a reasonable energy balance for the basis sets. 
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the function
$       with the largest exponent.
$ 
$ The valence space is defined here as the 6d, 7s, and 7p orbitals. 
$ 
$ 
$ 1. Valence flexibility. To the SCF functions, add the following:
$ 
$    1st and 3rd s primitives from the SCF set 
$    2nd and 3rd p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set
$    1st and 2nd f primitives from the SCF set (correlating f functions)
$    Valence correlating g function
$ 
$ 2. Valence + outer core. This set adds the outer-core correlation from the 6s and 6p
$    shells. To set 1, add the following functions:
$ 
$    4th and 6th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    5th and 6th p primitives from the SCF set
$    4th and 5th d primitives from the SCF set
$    3rd f primitive from the SCF set
$ 
$    If linear dependence is encountered in the d set, do not uncontract the 2nd primitive.
$ 
$ 3. Valence + outer core + 5f. This set adds correlation from the 5f shell. To set 2,
$    add the following functions:
$ 
$    2nd f primitive from the SCF set
$    Correlating 2g1h functions for 5f correlation
$ 
$    The s, p, and d correlation are adequately covered by set 2. If further flexibility in 
$    the f set is needed, uncontract the 4th f primitive.
$ 
$    (HF + 1s2p2d1f valence + 1s1p1d outer core + 1d2f1g core)
$ 
$ 4. Dipole polarization of the 6d. To any of the above basis sets, add the dipole polarizing
$    f and g functions.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

