$ Fr Ra
$ 
$ 7s Block Triple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ ======================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 5 Nov 2009
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, J. Phys. Chem. A (2009) 113:12638. Available from the Dirac web site, 
$ http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF orbital coefficients, given
$ with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the basis sets described in the above articles. 
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * Optimization of the s, p, d, and f sets separately, on the total energy of 
$   the ground state
$ * Optimization of 3 functions for the 7p of Fr on the 7p eigenvalues of the 7p1
$   configuration, followed by reoptimization on the total energy of the 7p1
$   configuration of the functions for the 3p-6p outer antinodes with the 7p
$   functions included and held fixed
$ * Optimization of 3 functions for the 7p of Ra along with reoptimization of the 
$   functions for the 6p outer antinode, on the total energy of the 7p2
$   configuration
$ * Replacement of the outermost d with the two 6p-correlating d functions and
$   reoptimization of the remainder.
$ * Replacement of the outermost two f functions with the 5d- and 6p-correlating 
$   f functions and reoptimization of the remainder.
$ 
$ In all the following descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF 
$ exponent set. 
$ 
$ The correlating functions were generated as follows
$ 
$ * Functions for the 6s6p and 5d shells were optimized in SDCI calculations with
$   excitations out of these shells coupled to J=0. Only the higher angular
$   functions were included in the correlating space (2d1f and 2f1g).
$ * The correlating functions for the 7s in Ra (2d1f) were optimized in MR-SDCI
$   calculations with the 7s and 7p orbitals in the active space, with the outer
$   6p-correlating d function included as one of the d functions and held fixed.
$ * The d function for the 7s in Fr was taken as 7/3 times the 2nd s exponent of
$   the SCF set; this ratio matches the mean radii of the functions.
$   
$ The functions for 6p dipole polarization were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * A polarizing s function was optimized on the polarizability of the 6p shell
$   with 6p -> ns excitations in the perturbing space. The uncontracted SCF s
$   functions (see below) for the 6s were included and their exponents were held 
$   fixed. 
$ * The ratio of the dipole polarizing s exponent to the outer SCF s exponent for
$   the 6s (4th exponent) was applied to the outer p exponent for the 6p (4th
$   exponent) to generate a p function.
$ * A polarizing d function was optimized on the polarizability of the 6p shell
$   with 6p -> nd excitations in the perturbing space. The 6p correlating d
$   functions were included and their exponents were held fixed. 
$ * The ratio of the dipole polarizing d exponent to the outer correlating d 
$   exponent was applied to the correlating f exponent for the 6p to generate an f
$   function.
$ 
$ The contractions are taken from SCF calculations on a weighted average of the
$ 7s^n and 7p^n configurations, with a weight ratio of 4:1.
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
$    of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
$    component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
$    symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
$    those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
$    The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
$    j=l+1/2.
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of 7s7p correlating functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of 6s6p correlating functions.
$ 
$ 5. Exponents of 5d correlating functions.
$ 
$ 6. Exponents of 6s6p dipole polarization functions
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ For the s elements, the outer core (n-1)s and (n-1)p must be included in the valence.
$ For Ra, the valence np is considered an occupied orbital and is correlated; hence 
$ there is an extra correlating d function for Ra. 
$ 
$ Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive 
$ functions to the SCF set. These basis sets are described below.
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ 1. Valence and outer core. To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
$    1st, 3rd, 4th, and 6th s primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 3rd, 4th, and 6th p primitives from the SCF set
$    6s6p correlating 2d1f functions (outermost 2d1f primitives from the SCF set)
$    valence correlating functions (1d for Fr, 1d1f for Ra)
$ 
$    (HF + 4s4p3d1f/2f valence/outer core)
$ 
$ 
$ 2. Core 5d correlation. To basis set 1, add the following:
$    7th and 9th s primitives from the SCF set
$    7th and 9th p primitives from the SCF set
$    3rd and 4th d primitives from the SCF set
$    5d correlating 2f1g set
$ 
$    (HF + 4s4p3d1f/2f valence/outer core + 2s2p2d2f1g core)
$ 
$ 
$ 3. Outer core (6s6p) dipole polarization. To any of the above basis sets, add the 
$    s, p, d, and f polarizing functions.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

